# Cevin: UI/UX simplification pass and Cover Letter feature

## 0. Context and ground rules
Cevin already exists and works. This task has two parts: (a) a UI/UX simplification and ergonomics pass across every screen, and (b) a new **Cover Letter** feature. Keep everything the codebase already guarantees:
- Hexagonal architecture with lint-enforced boundaries. Each extension is one adapter plus one registry line.
- Tailwind v4 with a single entry CSS file (`@import "tailwindcss"`, `@custom-variant`, `@theme`). Zero inline styles, no string-built class names, and feature code composes design-system primitives only.
- Truthfulness: every generated sentence carries `sourceIds`, unverified claims are flagged, and gaps are never silently filled.

Work in the phases in section 12, keep the app runnable, and commit per phase. Record decisions in `docs/DECISIONS.md`. Update the README (navigation description and screenshots), `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/EXTENDING.md` and `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`. The baseline viewport is 1440×900. Verify layouts at 1920×1080 and 2560×1440 as well.

## 1. Design intent
- **One way to get anywhere.** Three destinations, one navigation.
- **One primary action per screen.** Never show the same action twice.
- **Read first, edit on demand.** Show content as readable text, and open fields only when the user asks to edit.
- **Speak the user's language.** No implementation vocabulary in the UI.
- **Calm on big screens.** More room means more breathing space, not more content.

